<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hann. Münden</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hannoversch Münden</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hann. Münden is a town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werra">Werra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> rivers meet to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>. A medieval trading town with staple rights over river traffic, it belonged to the Guelph Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> and later to Calenberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>. Its old town of half-timbered houses and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> Guelph castle survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hann-munden.jpg" alt="Hann. Münden" width="320" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Axel Hindemith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hann_M%C3%BCnden_Rathaus_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
